messages:
alex: heads-up — saw a couple of reports from customers using our node ts SDK in Next and Vite apps. error is "Cannot find module '.../dist/node/index.cjs'" when they do a require() or when bundlers try to include the package. any quick ideas?

marta: likely exports/conditional-exports issue. are we shipping only ESM entry in package.json? what does package.json look like for the SDK (exports + main/module)?

sam: fwiw I ran into similar with a 3rd party that set only "exports" -> "./dist/index.mjs" and Node CJS consumers fail. simple fix is to add conditional exports for "require" and "import" and a legacy "main" fallback.

alex: here's snippet I suggested to a user that fixed things:

```json
"main": "dist/node/index.cjs",
"module": "dist/node/index.mjs",
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/node/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/node/index.cjs",
    "default": "./dist/node/index.cjs"
  }
}
```

marta: also add a top-level "types" pointing to the d.ts so ts consumers get good intellisense. and ensure your bundler consumers can resolve the correct file by not forcing the cjs file to be marked as "exports blocked".

sam: for Next specifically, if folks hit errors during edge build, sometimes the solution is to mark the package as external for the edge or transpile it. example next.config.js with next-transpile-modules:

```js
// next.config.js
const withTM = require('next-transpile-modules')(['@ourorg/sdk']);
module.exports = withTM({
  experimental: { esmExternals: true }
});
```

alex: I posted a small guide in the docs repo but here's a compact checklist for devs:
- ensure package.json has both import/require exports
- publish both cjs + esm builds (dist/node/* and dist/edge/*)
- for Vite: set optimizeDeps.include or ssr.noExternal if using SSR
- for Next edge: avoid Node builtins in edge builds; make an edge-compat wrapper

ci-bot: :white_check_mark: link to CI test that caught missing cjs export: https://ci.internal/1234

marta: Vite specifics — if users see "ReferenceError: global is not defined" during dev, usually bundler polyfills are the culprit. recommend shipping a small entry that uses `typeof globalThis !== 'undefined' ? globalThis : window` or add a browser field mapping for edge builds.

sam: streaming gotchas: edge runtimes return web ReadableStream while Node streaming returns Node.Readable. put a small helper in the SDK to normalise:```
export async function streamToAsyncIterable(body) {
  if (body.getReader) { // web ReadableStream
    const reader = body.getReader();
    return {
      async *[Symbol.asyncIterator]() {
        while (true) {
          const { done, value } = await reader.read();
          if (done) break;
          yield value;
        }
      }
    };
  }
  // Node stream
  return body;
}
```

alex: auth + retries quick notes: pass API key via env var or in process.env.RI_API_KEY; for client libs allow `new Client({ apiKey: process.env.RI_API_KEY })`. retry logic: idempotent by default on 429/503 with exponential backoff + jitter, but DO NOT retry non-idempotent POSTs unless user opts in.

marta: added to the internal TODO: create a one-page troubleshooting doc + npm tag `edge-compatible` for packages built only for edge. also add smoke tests in CI for both next + vite example apps.

sam: one last tip — if you see build-time errors like "unexpected token export" after bundling, check that the package was consumed as esm by the bundler. forcing the package to be transpiled (ssr.noExternal / transpilePackages) usually fixes it.

alex: thanks. i pinged @eng-runtime to consider adding a prepublish check that verifies exports contain both require/import entries and that edge bundle artifacts are present. will follow up in the doc. :thumbsup:
